<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Croÿ Tapestry</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1554</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Croÿ Tapestry is a large tapestry made in 1554 by Peter Heymans for Duke Philip I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>. It shows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> preaching before the families of the dukes of Pomerania and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, celebrating their marriage alliance and Protestant faith. It is named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernst-bogislaw-von-croy">Ernst Bogislaw von Croÿ</a>, the last heir of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-griffins">Griffin dynasty</a>, who left it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-greifswald">University of Greifswald</a>.</p></div>
